From: Daniel Lewis <dl3645@columbia.edu>
Sent: 19 November 2025 02:31
To: Thies Lindenthal
Subject: Re: Request to discuss research proposal

Absolutely and thank you for the quick response, Prof Lindenthal,


Thank you for clarifying your interests.


In your recent REIT paper with Kahshin Leow you show that ML can
extract cross-sectional expected return premia from a very
high-dimensional set of characteristics, and in your work with Wayne
Wan you use LIME to test image-based AVMs. I would like to build on
this by using attribution methods, made distributionally robust to
changes in the cross-section (across cities, property types, and
market regimes) as well as to long and sparse tails, to recover a
stable map from observable characteristics and frictions - for
example, zoning and planning regulations, liquidity, climate risk,
building features - to prices and expected returns.


Standard explanation methods such as LIME or SHAP can shift when you
resample the data or focus on thin, high-value or high-risk parts of
the distribution. Yet, for some, it is exactly in these long tails
that accurate explanations matters the most. Additionally, drivers
whose importance survives cross-sectional resampling may be treated as
structural across markets and regimes. As one concrete example, this
would facilitate testing where a pro forma differs in treatment to a
machine learning model in how it factors more strongly or weakly
features into prices and expected returns, and how the strength of
that driver varies depending on which part of the market you are in.

Let me know if this sounds aligned to your work, and if so when works
best to chat.


My best,
Daniel



